[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Anti-Patterns in Mocking: Over-mocking and Fragile Tests in Mockito"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Mocking frameworks like Mockito are essential tools in modern unit testing. However, when abused, they lead to anti-patterns that degrade test maintainability, hide architectural flaws, and create a false sense of security. This tutorial explores the pitfalls of over-mocking and fragile tests, demonstrating how to refactor toward robust unit testing."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"What is Over-Mocking?"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Over-mocking occurs when a test mocks nearly every collaborator of the class under test. Instead of testing business logic, the test ends up verifying interaction details and configuration wiring. This often stems from classes having too many responsibilities (violating the Single Responsibility Principle)."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Example of an Over-Mocked Test"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"Consider an OrderProcessor service that relies on multiple utility and data components. The following example demonstrates an over-mocked test that is tightly coupled to the implementation."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"@ExtendWith(MockitoExtension.class)
class OrderProcessorTest {
    @Mock private DatabaseClient dbClient;
    @Mock private Logger logger;
    @Mock private TaxCalculator taxCalculator;
    @Mock private EmailService emailService;
    @Mock private InventoryService inventoryService;

    @InjectMocks
    private OrderProcessor orderProcessor;

    @Test
    void testProcessOrder() {
        Order order = new Order("123", 100.0);
        when(taxCalculator.calculate(100.0)).thenReturn(10.0);
        when(inventoryService.reserve("123")).thenReturn(true);

        orderProcessor.process(order);

        verify(dbClient).save(order);
        verify(logger).info("Order processed successfully");
        verify(emailService).sendConfirmation(order);
        verify(inventoryService).reserve("123");
    }
}"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Why Fragile Tests Happen"},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Tightly Coupled to Implementation: Changing an internal log message or swapping a private collaborator breaks the test, even if the public output remains correct."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Maintenance Burden: Every time a dependency's signature changes, multiple mock setups and verifications must be manually updated."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Testing Mockito, Not Code: The test merely verifies that the mocked methods were called in the exact sequence expected, resembling a script rather than an assertion of business rules."},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices to Avoid Over-Mocking"},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use Real Objects for Value Objects and Simple Services: Do not mock simple domain objects, value holders, or deterministic utility classes like calculators."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Favor State Verification Over Interaction Verification: Instead of verifying that 'emailService.sendConfirmation()' was called, assert that the resulting database state or returned object contains the expected changes."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Limit the Number of Mocks: If a class requires more than three or four mocks, it is usually a smell that the class is doing too much and should be refactored."},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Refactored Clean Test Example"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"By replacing unnecessary mocks with real implementations of stateless utilities and focusing purely on the output behavior, we drastically reduce test fragility."},{"order":18,"type":"code","text":"@ExtendWith(MockitoExtension.class)
class OrderProcessorRefactoredTest {
    @Mock private DatabaseClient dbClient;
    @Mock private EmailService emailService;

    // Using a real instance of a pure calculation utility instead of a mock
    private final TaxCalculator taxCalculator = new StandardTaxCalculator();

    @InjectMocks
    private OrderProcessor orderProcessor;

    @Test
    void testProcessOrderSuccessfully() {
        Order order = new Order("123", 100.0);

        orderProcessor.process(order);

        // Verify the outcome rather than internal logging and intermediate calls
        assertEquals(110.0, order.getTotalWithTax());
        verify(dbClient).save(order);
        verify(emailService).sendConfirmation(order);
    }
}"},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"Mocking should be reserved for true boundary dependencies such as databases, external APIs, and asynchronous queues. Avoid mocking every internal collaborator, and always prioritize testing the actual state and outcomes over verifying every single method interaction."}]
